---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'AGM vs Flooded Battery: Sealed vs Liquid Electrolyte';
const description = 'AGM immobilises the electrolyte in a glass mat; flooded keeps it as free liquid. Sealed and maintenance-free vs cheaper and traditional — which fits which job.';
const canonical = '/lead-acid/agm-vs-flooded/';

const articleJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'Article',
  '@id': `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}#article`,
  headline: title,
  url: `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}`,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  about: 'AGM vs flooded battery',
  articleSection: 'Lead-Acid Battery Knowledge',
  dateModified: '2026-09-16',
  isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://worldbatteryhub.com/#website' },
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What is the difference between AGM and flooded batteries?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Flooded keeps the electrolyte as free liquid and vents during charging, needing occasional watering. AGM absorbs the electrolyte in a fiberglass mat inside a sealed valve-regulated case — maintenance-free, spill-proof and more vibration- and cycling-tolerant, at a higher price.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Can I replace a flooded battery with AGM?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Usually yes — AGM is a drop-in upgrade in most cars, and it is required rather than optional on many start-stop vehicles. The charging system should have an AGM-compatible profile for full life.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  author="World Battery Hub"
  dateModified="2026-09-16"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · Comparisons · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>
      <h1>AGM vs Flooded Battery: Sealed vs Liquid Electrolyte</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The oldest battery design and its sealed descendant sit side by side on the shelf — same chemistry,
        different packaging, and the packaging changes how each one lives.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> <strong>Flooded</strong> keeps the electrolyte as <strong>free liquid</strong>
        with vent caps, needs occasional watering, and costs less. <strong>AGM</strong> absorbs the electrolyte in a
        <strong>fiberglass mat</strong> inside a sealed
        <a href="https://technology.worldbatteryhub.com/vrla/">valve-regulated</a> case — maintenance-free,
        spill-proof, more vibration- and cycling-tolerant, at a higher price.
      </div>

      <h2>How do AGM and flooded compare?</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Flooded</th><th>AGM</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Electrolyte</strong></td><td>Free liquid, vent caps</td><td>Absorbed in glass mat, sealed</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Maintenance</strong></td><td>Occasional watering</td><td>None</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Vibration / tilt tolerance</strong></td><td>Limited</td><td>High</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Cycling tolerance</strong></td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Price</strong></td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Typical use</strong></td><td>Basic cars, budget</td><td>Start-stop, premium, demanding duty</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Why AGM is often not optional</h2>
      <p>
        Start-stop systems cycle the battery far more than a traditional car, and the repeated discharge while the
        engine is off demands the cycling tolerance that only AGM (or EFB as a step below) provides
        (see <a href="/lead-acid/efb-battery/">EFB</a>). In those cars, AGM is the specification, not an upgrade —
        installing a flooded battery there shortens its life sharply.
      </p>

      <h2>Which one should I choose?</h2>
      <p>
        Follow the car's requirement first: start-stop and demanding vehicles specify AGM. For a basic car, the
        choice is budget vs convenience — flooded wins on price and keeps working well in gentle duty, while AGM
        buys freedom from watering and greater tolerance of heat, vibration and shallow cycling. Neither is
        universally better; the fit decides (see <a href="/lead-acid/agm-vs-gel/">AGM vs gel</a> for the other
        sibling comparison).
      </p>
      <h2>The numbers</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Flooded</th><th>AGM</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Cycling endurance (relative)</td><td>1× (baseline)</td><td>~2–3×</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Maintenance</td><td>Occasional watering</td><td>None (sealed)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Price (same Ah class)</td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Installation orientation</td><td>Upright only</td><td>Most orientations</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">Order-of-magnitude comparisons — the duty (start-stop or not) usually decides before price does.</p>


      <h2>Our Interpretation</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>In my view:</strong> AGM vs flooded is a packaging decision with real consequences — the seal buys
          maintenance-freedom and cycling tolerance, the liquid buys cheapness — and the car's own specification
          usually makes the decision for you.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>What drives this:</strong> Where the duty is gentle and money matters, flooded still works as it has for a
          century; where the duty cycles and shakes, the sealed design earns its premium. Match the packaging to
          the duty, and the comparison resolves itself.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>My editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What is the difference between AGM and flooded batteries?</h3>
      <p>Flooded keeps the electrolyte as free liquid and vents during charging, needing occasional watering. AGM absorbs the electrolyte in a fiberglass mat inside a sealed valve-regulated case — maintenance-free, spill-proof and more vibration- and cycling-tolerant, at a higher price.</p>
      <h3>Can I replace a flooded battery with AGM?</h3>
      <p>Usually yes — AGM is a drop-in upgrade in most cars, and it is required rather than optional on many start-stop vehicles. The charging system should have an AGM-compatible profile for full life.</p>
<h2>What buyers should ask</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Does the application require a sealed, spill-proof, vibration-resistant battery, which favours AGM?</li>
  <li>Will the vehicle's charging system suit AGM's sensitivity to overcharge?</li>
  <li>Is the extra cycle life and CCA of AGM worth the premium for this duty?</li>
  <li>Does the mounting orientation or environment (vibration, tilt) rule out a vented flooded battery?</li>
</ul>
<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>AGM and flooded are the same chemistry in two packages — AGM sealed and higher-performing, flooded cheaper and vented. Choose by the vehicle's charging system and the duty's demand for vibration resistance and cycle life, not by the label alone.</p>

<h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Cross-verified from battery engineering references and manufacturer documentation.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Manufacturer documentation — flooded and AGM construction and duty guidance.</li>
        <li>Battery engineering references — VRLA design and cycling behaviour.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-16</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
